<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franco-Flemish War (1297–1305)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1297">1297</a>–<a href="/years/1305">1305</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franco-Flemish War was fought from 1297 to 1305 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> and Guy of Dampierre, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied Flanders, but the Flemish towns revolted and defeated the French knights at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> in 1302; after the French victory at Mons-en-Pévèle (1304), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-athis-sur-orge">Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge</a> (1305) imposed heavy terms on Flanders while leaving it autonomous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-flemish-war-1297-1305.jpg" alt="Franco-Flemish War (1297–1305)" width="320" height="110" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown sculptor, Chest of Courtrai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goedendag_on_chest_of_Kortrijk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1285 Philip IV sought to subject his great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> he backed the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> against their count. The war was bound up with the Gascon War of 1294–1303 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-scottish-independence">First War of Scottish Independence</a>. Guy's daughter Philippa was betrothed to the future Edward II, which Philip treated as treason: he imprisoned Guy until February 1295, blocked the English wool trade on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> depended, levied a new war tax and placed royal supervisors in the towns. On 7 January 1297 Guy, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>, renounced his vassalage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Edward, faced with opposition at home and the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, gave little help. A French army under Charles of Valois besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, Robert of Artois won the Battle of Furnes on 20 August 1297, and Lille, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> fell. A papal truce on 12 October let Edward withdraw, and the Treaty of Montreuil of 1299 left Guy without allies. When the war resumed in 1300 the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damme">Damme</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, and on 24 May Guy and his sons Robert and William were led into captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>The governor Jacques de Châtillon and the Leliaards, the French king's Flemish supporters, raised new taxes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> allied with the nobles loyal to the count. The militia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> massacred French troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matins-of-bruges">Matins of Bruges</a>, and Guy of Namur and William of Jülich led the revolt. On 11 July 1302 the Flemish militia defeated the French cavalry of Robert II of Artois near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a>, and by November the rebels held the whole county. They won again at Arques in April 1303, but in August 1304 a Franco-Hollandic fleet defeated Guy of Namur at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zierikzee">Zierikzee</a>, and the two armies met at Mons-en-Pévèle on 18 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A truce was made at the abbey of Marquette on 23 September 1304, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-athis-sur-orge">Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge</a> was signed on 23 June 1305. It laid exorbitant fines on the Flemish towns and restored the count as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the French king. Philip probably restored him because French rule in 1300–1302 had failed and he needed someone able to collect the fines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franco-Flemish War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
